R=[
N('castolon-historic-district','Castolon Historic District','Big Bend National Park','tx','town','standing',29.1342,-103.5142,'site','public','drive','','1901–1961',
  'Cipriano Hernandez farmed the river bottom here from 1901 and ran the first store out of his house, now called the Alvino House after Alvino Ybarra, who lived in it from 1918 to 1957. During the Mexican Revolution the Army set up Camp Santa Helena (1916), first in tents; permanent buildings went up in 1919 and 1920, but the border had gone quiet and the soldiers probably never moved in. The La Harmonia Company store took over the empty barracks and traded on both sides of the river until the National Park Service bought the holdings in 1961. A fire that crossed the Rio Grande from Mexico on May 22, 2019 badly damaged the store and the old latrine. The year the store moved into the barracks is not confirmed: one park page gives 1921, another says the company bought the buildings in 1925.',
  'The Army buildings on the bluff, with the fire-damaged barracks store and latrine closed. The Garlick House is now the visitor center. Below the bluff is the Alvino House, the oldest intact adobe in the park (the park gives about 1901 and about 1903), restored in 1994 by adobe masons from Santa Helena, Mexico. There is also a second store building, Old Castolon, across from Cottonwood Campground, adobe ruins of farm homes, and two cemeteries.',
  'A cavalry post nobody lived in became the trading post for both sides of the river.',
  BIBE+' Castolon is at the south end of the Ross Maxwell Scenic Drive, about 22 miles from its northern end, with parking at the district. The Castolon visitor center opens November to April, daily 9 a.m. to 4 p.m., closed noon to 1. The burned store and latrine are closed.',
  [['NPS: History of the Castolon area','https://www.nps.gov/bibe/learn/historyculture/castolon_history.htm'],
   ['NPS: Castolon Historic District','https://www.nps.gov/places/castolon-historic-district.htm'],
   ['NPS: Alvino House','https://www.nps.gov/places/alvino-house.htm'],
   ['NPS: La Harmonia Store','https://www.nps.gov/bibe/learn/historyculture/laharmonia.htm'],W('Castolon'),L_HOURS,L_FEES],conf='mixed',th=[29.1335,-103.51429],thn='Castolon parking, Ross Maxwell Scenic Drive'),
N('rancho-estelle-dorgan-sublett','Rancho Estelle (Dorgan and Sublett farms)','Big Bend National Park','tx','town','ruins',29.15697,-103.57548,'exact','public','walk','','1918–1941',
  'James and Melissa Belle Sublett came to Castolon in 1913. In 1918 Sublett bought four sections of river bottom here and called it Grand Canyon Farms, later Rancho Estelle; he leveled the fields with mule-drawn scrapers left from his years as a railroad earth-moving contractor, and was one of the first to farm the Rio Grande floodplain on a large scale. In the 1930s the architect Albert Dorgan and his wife Avis Ann bought land next door and built an adobe house on the mesa, with a petrified-wood fireplace in the middle that carried the roof beams, so the house could be twice as wide as others nearby. The Dorgans left in 1938; a resort planned on their land in 1941 failed, and both farms went into the park. Sublett\'s adobe house was torn down when the park took it, before anyone counted it as history.',
  'Four structures on a half-mile trail: a stone farmhouse at the trailhead with an exhibit, the small adobe La Casita, the foundation of the Sublett house, and the Dorgan house, whose fireplace and part of whose walls still stand, looking toward Santa Elena Canyon and the Chisos.',
  'An architect\'s house on a mesa, held up by a petrified-wood chimney.',
  BIBE+' The Dorgan-Sublett Trail starts at a dirt pullout for two vehicles on the Ross Maxwell Scenic Drive, about 4.5 miles west of Castolon: 1 mile round trip with one 56-foot climb. The Sublett house ground is fragile; stay on the trail.',
  [['NPS: Dorgan-Sublett Trail','https://www.nps.gov/places/dorgan-sublett-trail.htm'],
   ['NPS: Dorgan House exhibit','https://www.nps.gov/places/dorgan-house-exhibit.htm'],
   ['NPS: Sublett House exhibit','https://www.nps.gov/places/sublett-house-exhibit.htm'],W('Rancho Estelle'),L_FEES],th=[29.15418,-103.57956],thn='Dorgan-Sublett trailhead pullout'),
N('lunas-jacal','Luna\'s Jacal','Big Bend National Park','tx','landmark','standing',29.21548,-103.53491,'site','public','drive','','',
  'The home of Gilberto Luna, a Mexican farmer who kept goats, farmed along Alamo Creek and sold firewood cut in the Chisos. A jacal is a low dugout house: this one backs onto a big boulder and had a roof of ocotillo stems weighted with earth and stones. Luna raised a large family here and died in 1947, at 108 or 109 by the Wikipedia article. When he came is not settled: the park says he arrived in 1916; the Wikipedia article gives a building date of 1890 in one place and about 1900 in another, and notes doubt about the old story that he lived here while the Comanche war trail was in use. The house was restored in 1971 and stabilized again in 1983.',
  'The low stone and earth house against its boulder, with a wayside panel about Luna and his family.',
  'A man who lived to 108 in a house half built into a rock.',
  BIBE+' At the foot of Pena Mountain, about 6 miles from the southern end of the Maverick Road, a gravel road that can become impassable after rain. Parking was not located on the aerial; park on the road shoulder.',
  [['NPS: Luna\'s Jacal','https://www.nps.gov/places/lunas-jacal.htm'],W('Luna Jacal'),L_FEES],conf='mixed'),
N('sam-nail-ranch','Sam Nail Ranch','Big Bend National Park','tx','town','ruins',29.27979,-103.3704,'site','public','walk','','1916–',
  'In 1916 Sam Nail built a two-room adobe house on the banks of Cottonwood Creek. He married soon after and raised a family here. A wooden windmill pumped water up to a tank on top of Burro Mesa for the Nails\' cattle. When the family left was not found in the sources read.',
  'The adobe walls of the house, slowly melting back into the ground, an orchard of pecan, walnut, willow and soapberry trees, the chicken coop, and the windmill, still pumping water that spills out and draws birds and other animals.',
  'A homestead where the windmill still runs.',
  BIBE+' A pullout on the right of the Ross Maxwell Scenic Drive, 3.4 miles south of its northern end, marks the trailhead; it fills at busy times. A half-mile loop on a rocky dirt path, with steps at both ends.',
  [['NPS: Sam Nail Ranch','https://www.nps.gov/places/sam-nail-ranch.htm'],L_FEES],th=[29.27931,-103.36865],thn='Sam Nail Ranch pullout, Ross Maxwell Scenic Drive'),
N('homer-wilson-ranch','Homer Wilson Ranch line camp','Big Bend National Park','tx','town','standing',29.21439,-103.36643,'exact','public','walk','','1928–1944',
  'Homer Wilson bought land in the foothills of the Chisos for sheep and goats, the first such large operation in the Big Bend; the park gives 1928 and the Wikipedia article 1929. The ranch grew to about 45,000 acres. This line camp in the Blue Creek valley, below Signal (or Carousel) Mountain, was built for the foreman, Lott Felts. Wilson died in 1943, the state had bought the ranch for the coming park in 1942, and the family moved off in 1944.',
  'A ranch house with a shady porch, a round corral with a snubbing post in the middle, dipping vats for sheep and goats, an old chicken coop and other ranch remains half hidden in brush.',
  '',
  BIBE+' The Homer Wilson Ranch overlook is 7.7 miles from the northern end of the Ross Maxwell Scenic Drive, with parking and a wheelchair-accessible wayside. From there a trail of about a mile round trip follows the old road 200 feet down to the camp; it crosses two drainages that can flood in the rainy season.',
  [['NPS: Homer Wilson Ranch overlook','https://www.nps.gov/places/homer-wilson-ranch-overlook.htm'],
   ['NPS: Homer Wilson Ranch trail','https://www.nps.gov/places/homer-wilson-ranch-trail.htm'],W('Homer Wilson Ranch'),L_FEES],conf='mixed',th=[29.21655,-103.36939],thn='Homer Wilson Ranch overlook'),
N('johnson-ranch-big-bend','Johnson\'s Ranch and airfield','Big Bend National Park','tx','town','ruins',29.0236,-103.37004,'area','public','drive','','1927–1943',
  'Elmo and Ada Johnson set up a ranch and trading post on the Rio Grande in 1927, sixteen miles downriver from Castolon. After bandits drove off their stock on April 11, 1929, during Mexico\'s Escobar Rebellion, the Army Air Corps opened a landing field here on April 24. The ranch house served as the field\'s headquarters and Johnson, a civilian, kept it running; by 1939 it had three graded runways, the longest 4,200 feet. Flying here peaked in 1936 and the field stayed in use until 1943. The place was also a cotton farm and goat ranch into the 1940s.',
  'The ruins of the Johnson ranch, a five-minute walk from the campsites, with a wide view of the Rio Grande, the south Chisos and the Sierra Ponce cliffs in Mexico. What stands of the house and the airstrip was not checked.',
  'An Army airfield run from a trading post porch.',
  BIBE+' On the River Road, 15.6 miles from its west end near Castolon (38.4 from the east end), then a 0.3-mile spur to the Johnson Ranch campsites. The River Road is for high-clearance vehicles only and can be impassable after rain; check with a ranger. No camping near the ruins.',
  [['NPS: Johnson\'s Ranch','https://www.nps.gov/bibe/learn/historyculture/johnsonsranch.htm'],
   ['NPS: Johnson Ranch campsites','https://www.nps.gov/bibe/planyourvisit/campsites_johnsonranch.htm'],L_FEES]),
N('mariscal-mine','Mariscal Mine','Big Bend National Park','tx','industry','ruins',29.09535,-103.18755,'site','public','drive','','1900–1943',
  'The rancher Martin Solis found cinnabar, the red ore of mercury, here in 1900. Several owners tried their luck: the Texas Almaden company from 1905, W. K. Ellis during the First World War, and from 1919 the Mariscal Mining Company, which built a brick Scott furnace and a row of condensers and closed in 1923. The Vivianna Mining Company tried again with a rotary furnace in the Second World War. In all the mine produced about 1,400 flasks of mercury, nearly a quarter of the country\'s output in those years. The miners were Mexicans who had crossed the river to escape the revolution, paid $1 to $1.50 a shift; many were poisoned by mercury. The last closing is not confirmed: the park gives 1943, the Wikipedia article 1944.',
  'The ruins of the Scott furnace with the concrete condensers above it, the early Ellis ore bin and retorts at the lowest level, stone miners\' houses of 1919 to 1923, and ten concrete houses of 1942 to 1943 that were probably never lived in. The park has a self-guiding story map of the trail through the mine.',
  'Where a quarter of America\'s mercury came out of one mountain.',
  BIBE+' About 22 miles down the River Road from its east end, west of Rio Grande Village. A high-clearance vehicle (at least 8 inches) is always required, and four-wheel drive after rain; ask a ranger about the road first and allow half a day. Stay off the structures; do not handle furnace bricks or tailings, which can hold poisonous mercury; mine shafts are deep and not all are fenced.',
  [['NPS: Mariscal Mine','https://www.nps.gov/bibe/learn/historyculture/mariscalmine.htm'],
   ['NPS: Mariscal Mine place page','https://www.nps.gov/places/mariscal-mine.htm'],W('Mariscal Mine'),L_FEES],conf='mixed'),
N('glenn-springs-big-bend','Glenn Springs','Big Bend National Park','tx','town','traces',29.17678,-103.1579,'area','public','drive','','1914–1920',
  'A reliable spring on a branch of the Comanche Trail. In 1914 C. D. Wood and W. K. Ellis built a factory here to boil candelilla wax out of the desert plant, and a village of nearly 80 people grew round it with a store and post office. On the night of May 5, 1916 raiders attacked; nine cavalrymen of the 14th Cavalry held an adobe building until its roof was set on fire. Three soldiers and the storekeeper\'s nine-year-old son were killed. The park says the raiders\' leader and loyalties are uncertain; the Wikipedia article calls them Pancho Villa\'s troops. A cavalry camp stayed until 1920; after the wax price fell the owners sold in 1919 and the village emptied.',
  'The spring, which still flows, scattered remnants of buildings and a cemetery. Watch for broken glass and metal.',
  'The 1916 border raid that brought the National Guard to the Big Bend.',
  BIBE+' Glenn Spring is 8.8 miles down the Glenn Spring Road, about an hour each way on rough gravel; the stretch south of the Juniper Canyon Road is very rough. Two primitive campsites (permit needed) are nearby.',
  [['NPS: Glenn Springs','https://www.nps.gov/bibe/learn/historyculture/glsp.htm'],
   ['NPS: Glenn Springs campsites','https://www.nps.gov/bibe/planyourvisit/campsites_glennsprings.htm'],W('Glenn Springs, Texas'),L_FEES],conf='mixed'),
N('hot-springs-big-bend','Hot Springs (Langford\'s resort)','Big Bend National Park','tx','hotel','ruins',29.1776,-102.9987,'site','public','walk','','1909–1952',
  'J. O. Langford, sick with malaria since childhood, claimed this spring by homestead in 1909 without having seen it, came with his family, took a 21-day cure, and then opened it to bathers at 10 cents a day. Bandit raids drove the family away in 1912. They came back in 1927, rebuilt the bathhouse, and built a store and post office and a motor court of seven cabins. Maggie Smith ran the business from the 1930s, after the park was founded too; the post office and resort closed in 1952.',
  'Langford\'s bathhouse is gone; the 105°F spring wells up inside its foundation at the edge of the Rio Grande and can be used for a soak. The store and post office and the motor court still stand, and there are pictographs on the cliff along the trail.',
  'A homesteader\'s cure that became the Big Bend\'s first tourist resort.',
  BIBE+' The Hot Springs Road is 2 miles of rough gravel down a narrow wash; motor homes and oversize vehicles are not allowed on its one-way parts. From the parking area it is half a mile round trip to the spring, with a 1-mile loop on the bluff. Soaking only, no soap; no glass or alcohol; no camping. River floods often bury the spring.',
  [['NPS: Hot Springs Historic District','https://www.nps.gov/bibe/learn/historyculture/hotsprings.htm'],
   ['NPS: Hot Springs place page','https://www.nps.gov/places/hot-springs.htm'],
   ['NPS: Soak in the hot spring','https://www.nps.gov/bibe/planyourvisit/soakinthesprings.htm'],W('Hot Springs (Big Bend National Park)'),L_FEES],th=[29.1776,-102.99941],thn='Hot Springs parking'),
N('daniels-ranch-big-bend','Daniels Farm House','Big Bend National Park','tx','town','standing',29.18584,-102.97207,'exact','public','drive','','1920s–1944',
  'John O. Wedlin, a Swedish immigrant who had grown wheat in Kansas, bought this river bottom from Jesus Estrada, leveled the fields, put in pumped irrigation, and built this adobe as a shed for farm equipment. He sold in 1926 to the cattleman Joe M. Graham, who fed his herd on the grain. In 1937 John R. and Mary Coe Daniels bought the northern part, moved into the shed and added a room for a small store, and grew cotton. They left when the park was founded in 1944.',
  'The two-room adobe, the last building left of the farm, kept up by the park, and the irrigation ditches, which run from it east through the campground.',
  '',
  BIBE+' One mile west of the Rio Grande Village campground, with parking, picnic tables and an accessible toilet.',
  [['NPS: Daniels Ranch','https://www.nps.gov/places/daniels-ranch.htm'],W('Daniels Farm House'),L_FEES]),
N('boquillas-ore-tramway','Boquillas ore tramway and terminal','Big Bend National Park','tx','industry','ruins',29.2186,-102.9496,'area','public','hike','','',
  'An aerial tramway carried ore from the Puerto Rico mine at Boquillas, in Mexico, across the river and over the desert to a terminal on a ridge above Ernst Valley. From there wagons took it to the railroad at Marathon on a road built for the purpose. There were two tramways; the earlier, smaller one was near today\'s Port of Entry. The dates of the tramways were not found in the park pages read.',
  'Footings of the first tower face an exhibit on the Boquillas Canyon Road. Remains of the larger line run along the Ore Terminal Trail to the terminal.',
  'An ore line in the sky from a Mexican mine to a Texas ridge.',
  BIBE+' The tramway exhibit is 3.5 miles down the Boquillas Canyon Road. The Ore Terminal Trail starts at the Marufo Vega trailhead, 2.5 miles down the same road (a dirt lot for about 5 cars): about 8 miles round trip and 800 feet of climbing on loose rock, best below 80°F and not a summer hike.',
  [['NPS: Ore tramway exhibit','https://www.nps.gov/places/ore-tramway-wayside.htm'],
   ['NPS: Marufo Vega and Ore Terminal trails','https://www.nps.gov/places/marufo-vega-ore-terminal-trails.htm'],L_FEES]),
N('contrabando-movie-set','Contrabando movie set','Lajitas','tx','amuse','standing',29.27944,-103.84111,'site','public','drive','','1985–2015',
  'A false western town built in 1985 for the Roy Clark film Uphill All the Way, around an older adobe called La Casita. Nine films were shot here, among them John Sayles\'s Lone Star (1996) and the Lonesome Dove films Dead Man\'s Walk and Streets of Laredo. A flood in September 2008 damaged the set, and in 2015 everything but La Casita was taken down for safety.',
  'La Casita, the original adobe, and the open ground where the set stood beside the Rio Grande.',
  'A Hollywood ghost town that is now really a ghost.',
  BBRSP+' The site is on FM 170 (the River Road), west of Lajitas. Where to park was not checked.',
  [W('Contrabando'),L_BBRSP]),
N('fort-leaton','Fort Leaton','Presidio','tx','fort','standing',29.542511,-104.326348,'exact','open','drive','','1848–1967',
  'An adobe house, thought to have been built in the 1830s by Juan Bustillos, that Benjamin Leaton bought in 1848 and turned into a fortified trading post. Leaton was a freighter on the Chihuahua Trail and a scalp hunter paid by Mexican governments; he also traded arms to Apache and Comanche raiders for stolen cattle. It was the first seat of Presidio County in 1850. After Leaton died in 1851 it passed through murder, debt and abandonment, until it was deeded to the state in 1967 and opened as a state historic site in 1978.',
  'The restored adobe fort, with walls 18 to 22 feet high, the family\'s living quarters, and exhibits. It is also the west entrance and visitor center of Big Bend Ranch State Park.',
  'A trading post kept by a scalp hunter.',
  'Fort Leaton State Historic Site, Texas Parks and Wildlife Department, 16952 FM 170 East, Presidio. Open daily, 8 a.m. to 4:30 p.m., closed December 25; $5 a day for anyone 13 or older.',
  [W('Fort Leaton State Historic Site'),['TPWD: Fort Leaton State Historic Site','https://tpwd.texas.gov/state-parks/fort-leaton'],L_BBRSP]),
N('gage-hotel-marathon','Gage Hotel','Marathon','tx','hotel','standing',30.20695,-103.24657,'exact','open','drive','','1927–',
  'The rancher Alfred S. Gage had the El Paso architect Henry Trost build this hotel in Marathon; it opened in 1927 and was also Gage\'s office until he died a year later. J. P. Bryan bought it in 1978 for $30,000 and renovated and enlarged it from the 1990s. It is on the National Register.',
  'The 1927 hotel building, still a hotel.',
  '',
  'A working private hotel: see it from the street, or go in as a customer. Hours were not checked.',
  [W('Marathon, Texas'),W('National Register of Historic Places listings in Brewster County, Texas')]),
N('kokernot-field','Kokernot Field','Alpine','tx','landmark','standing',30.3727,-103.66463,'exact','view','drive','','1947–',
  'A baseball park built in 1947 by the rancher Herbert Lee Kokernot Jr. for his semiprofessional team, with red infield clay brought by boxcar from Georgia and a grandstand and outfield wall of stone quarried on his ranch. The ranch brand and iron baseballs with painted stitches decorate it. About 6,000 people came in 1951 to see Satchel Paige\'s St. Louis Browns play the Chicago White Sox. Lights came in 1958, the Alpine school district took it over in 1968, and it was put on the National Register in 2026.',
  'The stone grandstand and outfield wall and the ironwork; it is still used by Sul Ross State University and the Alpine Cowboys.',
  'A cattleman\'s stone ballpark in the desert.',
  'Owned by the Alpine Independent School District. See it from the street, or go to a game; hours and visiting rules were not checked.',
  [W('Kokernot Field')]),
]
